# Tutorial 17 — Inline Java in VNS
# $ shorthand, [java] blocks, @jimport, @bind, [init java], and [java class].

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_17_inline_java
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi default]
[set ui.textBoxAsset default]
[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[set java_points 0]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: This lesson is about the escape hatch: writing {b}real Java code{/b} directly inside a .vns script.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Most scenes should stay in VNS. Use Java when you need custom calculations, engine integration, debugging helpers, or logic that would be awkward in command syntax.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> When to use Java inside VNS -> section_when
> $ shorthand — one-line Java -> section_dollar
> [java] blocks — multi-line code -> section_block
> Vn facade — script-friendly helpers -> section_facade
> @bind — bridge VNS variables into Java -> section_bind
> @jimport, [init java], and [java class] -> section_reuse
> Quick reference: Java syntax -> quick_reference
> Practice: live Java quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Inline Java comes in a few forms:
narrator: {b}$ oneLineJava;{/b} — quick one-line execution.
narrator: {b}[java] ... [/java]{/b} — multi-line code block.
narrator: {b}@jimport package.Type{/b} — add imports for later Java blocks.
narrator: {b}@bind int:score{/b} — read a VNS variable into Java, then write it back after the block.
narrator: {b}[java class Name] ... [/java]{/b} — define a reusable helper class for this scenario.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Rule of thumb: if the player should read it, write VNS. If the machine should calculate it, Java is fair game.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_when
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── When to Use Java ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_when
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: VNS is optimized for story flow: dialogue, choices, variables, characters, audio, transitions.
Lavender: Java is optimized for custom logic: loops, collections, reusable calculations, integrations, and debugging.
narrator: Good Java use cases:
narrator: {b}Scoring{/b} a complex route from many variables.
narrator: {b}Formatting{/b} a dynamic value before showing it.
narrator: {b}Calling{/b} a Java helper already in your project.
narrator: {b}Debugging{/b} state while developing.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Bad use case: replacing every simple [set], [show], and [jump] with Java. That makes your script harder to read.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Think of Java as a precise tool, not the default language for the whole scene.
> Next: $ shorthand -> section_dollar
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── $ Shorthand ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_dollar
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A line that starts with {b}$ {/b} runs one line of Java immediately.
narrator: Example:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}$ Vn.setVar("debug_seen_java", true);{/color}
$ Vn.setVar("debug_seen_java", true);
[if debug_seen_java == true]
Lavender: That line just set {b}debug_seen_java{/b} to true, and this [if] block can see it.
[endif]
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The $ form is best for tiny actions: setting a computed value, logging, or showing a quick HUD message.
$ Vn.hud("One-line Java ran from the VNS script.", 1400);
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: If the code needs more than one line, switch to a [java] block so it stays readable.
> Next: [java] blocks -> section_block
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [java] Blocks ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_block
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: A {b}[java]{/b} block preserves everything until {b}[/java]{/b}: blank lines, comments, loops, and normal Java statements.
narrator: This block builds a short summary with a loop, then stores it in a VNS variable.
[java]
StringBuilder summary = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    if (i > 1) summary.append(", ");
    summary.append("step ").append(i);
}
Vn.setVar("java_summary", summary.toString());
Vn.setVar("java_summary_ready", true);
[/java]
[if java_summary_ready == true]
Lavender: Java built a summary string and stored it in VNS state.
[endif]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Notice that the block used normal Java syntax, including a loop and StringBuilder.
Lavender: The result crossed back into VNS through {b}Vn.setVar{/b}, then the dialogue line displayed it with a variable tag.
> Next: Vn facade -> section_facade
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Vn Facade ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_facade
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Inside inline Java, the {b}Vn{/b} facade gives you script-friendly helpers.
narrator: Common calls:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Vn.setVar("key", value);{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Vn.intVar("score", 0);{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Vn.strVar("name", "Player");{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Vn.show("lavender", "happy", "center");{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Vn.hud("message", 1200);{/color}
[java]
int current = Vn.intVar("java_points", 0);
Vn.setVar("java_points", current + 2);
Vn.hud("Vn facade added 2 java_points.", 1400);
[/java]
[show lavender center happy]
[if java_points >= 2]
Lavender: The current Java point total is at least 2, so this [if] branch can see the value Java wrote.
[endif]
Lavender: You can also use the lower-level {b}scene{/b} and {b}state{/b} variables, but Vn is cleaner for most script work.
> Next: @bind -> section_bind
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── @bind ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_bind
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}@bind{/b} creates a typed Java local variable from a VNS variable.
narrator: This file declares {color=#8888cc}@bind int:java_points{/color} before the block.
@bind int:java_points
[java]
java_points += 5;
[/java]
[show lavender center happy]
[if java_points >= 7]
Lavender: Java changed the local variable, and @bind wrote it back. The value is now high enough for this VNS condition.
[endif]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Supported common types include int, long, double, float, boolean, and String.
Lavender: Use @bind when you want Java code to read and write a VNS variable without repeating Vn.getVar and Vn.setVar.
> Next: reusable Java -> section_reuse
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Imports, Init Blocks, and Classes ───────────────────────────

@label section_reuse
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For reusable code, VNS supports imports, init blocks, and scenario-scoped Java classes.
narrator: First, {b}@jimport{/b} adds imports to later Java blocks.
@jimport java.time.LocalTime
[java]
Vn.setVar("java_clock_hour", LocalTime.now().getHour());
Vn.setVar("java_import_ready", true);
[/java]
[if java_import_ready == true]
Lavender: Java imported LocalTime and stored the current hour in VNS state.
[endif]
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[init java]{/b} compiles setup code and optionally runs an init method.
[init java]
public static void init(VnScene scene) {
    scene.getState().setVariable("java_init_ready", true);
}
[/init]
[if java_init_ready == true]
Lavender: The init method ran and set {b}java_init_ready{/b}.
[endif]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[java class Name]{/b} defines a reusable helper class for this scenario.
[java class TutorialJavaMath]
public static int clamp(int value, int min, int max) {
    return Math.max(min, Math.min(max, value));
}
[/java]
[java]
int rawScore = 14;
Vn.setVar("java_clamped_score", TutorialJavaMath.clamp(rawScore, 0, 10));
[/java]
[show lavender center happy]
[if java_clamped_score == 10]
Lavender: The helper class clamped 14 down to 10, then VNS reacted to that result.
[endif]
Lavender: Put repeated calculations in a class. Keep one-off story actions in [java] blocks.
> Practice: live Java quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[set java_quiz_score 0]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Quick check. Three questions about Java inside VNS.

@label jq1
Lavender: Q1: Which form is best for one tiny Java statement?
> $ shorthand -> jq1_correct
> [java class Name] -> jq1_wrong
> @include -> jq1_wrong

@label jq1_correct
[inc java_quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Correct. $ is the compact one-line form.
[jump jq2]

@label jq1_wrong
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Not quite. A class is for reusable helpers; @include is for VNS files. Use $ for a tiny one-liner.
[jump jq2]

@label jq2
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Q2: What does @bind int:score do?
> Bridges a VNS variable into Java and writes it back -> jq2_correct
> Creates a new character sprite -> jq2_wrong
> Starts background music -> jq2_wrong

@label jq2_correct
[inc java_quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Right. @bind gives Java a typed local variable connected to VNS state.
[jump jq3]

@label jq2_wrong
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: @bind is for variables, not visuals or audio.
[jump jq3]

@label jq3
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Q3: When should you prefer normal VNS over Java?
> Dialogue, choices, and simple scene commands -> jq3_correct
> Complex loops and helper calculations -> jq3_wrong
> Calling existing Java systems -> jq3_wrong

@label jq3_correct
[inc java_quiz_score]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Exactly. Keep story flow readable in VNS.
[jump java_quiz_result]

@label jq3_wrong
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Those are the cases where Java helps. Plain story flow should stay in VNS.
[jump java_quiz_result]

@label java_quiz_result
[show lavender center talking]
[if java_quiz_score >= 3]
Lavender: 3/3. You know when to reach for Java and when to leave the script alone.
[elif java_quiz_score == 2]
Lavender: 2/3. Good enough to start experimenting safely.
[elif java_quiz_score == 1]
Lavender: 1/3. Review the quick reference before using Java in production scripts.
[else]
Lavender: 0/3. Start with $ and [java] blocks, then come back to the advanced parts.
[endif]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Inline Java is powerful because it stays close to the scene that needs it. Use it sparingly and comment the intent.
> Retake the quiz -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
